---
import GuideLayout from '@layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import { config } from './config';
import type { EcosystemEntry } from '@data/types';

interface Props {
  title: string;
  description: string;
  readMinutes: number;
  updatedAt: string;
  entry: EcosystemEntry;
}
const props = Astro.props;
---

<GuideLayout
  {...props}
  toolSlug={config.slug}
  toolName={config.name}
  toolCategorySlug={config.categorySlug}
>
  <section id="overview">
    <h2>Overview</h2>
    <p>
      Contrast is what makes text legible. If the letters are too close in brightness to the surface
      behind them, readers with low vision, aging eyes, or a sunlit phone screen simply cannot make
      them out. The Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) turn that into a measurable number:
      the contrast ratio between a text color and its background. This tool computes that ratio and
      tells you which accessibility levels the pair passes.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="the-ratio">
    <h2>How the contrast ratio works</h2>
    <p>
      Every color has a relative luminance, a value from 0 (black) to 1 (white) that models how
      bright it looks to the human eye. Green contributes most to that brightness and blue the least.
      The contrast ratio takes the lighter color's luminance plus 0.05, divided by the darker color's
      luminance plus 0.05. The result runs from 1 to 1, when the two colors are identical, up to 21
      to 1 for pure black on pure white. A higher number means easier reading.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="thresholds">
    <h2>The AA and AAA thresholds</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>AA, normal text:</strong> at least 4.5 to 1. This is the level most laws and design systems require.</li>
      <li><strong>AA, large text:</strong> at least 3 to 1, because bigger letters stay readable at lower contrast.</li>
      <li><strong>AAA, normal text:</strong> at least 7 to 1, the enhanced level for maximum readability.</li>
      <li><strong>AAA, large text:</strong> at least 4.5 to 1.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Large text means about 24px for regular weight or about 18.66px when bold. The checker shows all
      four verdicts side by side so you never have to remember which threshold applies.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="how-to">
    <h2>Checking and fixing a color pair</h2>
    <p>
      Enter your text color and background color, by typing a value or dragging the picker. The ratio
      and the four pass or fail badges update as you go, and the preview panel shows real sample text
      in your colors. If the text color fails AA, press Suggest an accessible text color: the tool
      keeps your hue and saturation and finds the nearest lightness that reaches 4.5 to 1, so your
      color stays on brand while becoming readable.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="beyond-contrast">
    <h2>Contrast is necessary, not sufficient</h2>
    <p>
      Passing every contrast check is a real win, but it is one slice of accessibility. Make sure
      information is never carried by color alone, that keyboard focus is visible, that interactive
      elements have clear labels, and that content works with a screen reader. Use the ratio as a
      firm minimum, then test the rest of the experience.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="privacy">
    <h2>Runs entirely in your browser</h2>
    <p>
      No color you enter leaves your device. The math runs locally, nothing is uploaded, and the tool
      works offline once loaded, so you can check contrast even inside an internal design tool with no
      internet access.
    </p>
  </section>
</GuideLayout>
